Each ported Rust function documents the XFOIL routine it derives from.

The vortex lattice of crates/vlm is a Rust port of algorithms from AVL 3.52
(https://web.mit.edu/drela/Public/web/avl/), Copyright (C) 2002 Mark Drela
and Harold Youngren, also distributed under the GNU General Public License,
version 2 or later:

  - amake.f    (MAKESURF, SDUPL, ENCALC)
  - aic.f      (VVOR, VORVELC)
  - asetup.f   (SETUP, GUCALC, GAMSUM, VELSUM)
  - aero.f     (AERO, SFFORC, VINFAB)
  - atpforc.f  (TPFORC)
  - aoutput.f  (DERMATS)
  - sgutil.f   (AKIMA, SPACER, CSPACER, NRMLIZ)
  - airutil.f  (GETCAM, LEFIND, NORMIT, READBL)
  - spline.f   (SPLIND, SEGSPL, SEVAL, DEVAL, D2VAL, SINVRT, SCALC)
  - ainput.f   (the geometry file format)

Airfoil coordinates of the UIUC Airfoil Coordinates Database (Michael
Selig, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign,
https://m-selig.ae.illinois.edu/ads/coord_database.html) are used as test
data (tests/) and in the airfoil catalogue of the application
(app/src-tauri/data/catalogue): AG35 (Mark Drela), Clark Y, E387 and E423
(Richard Eppler), FX 63-137 (Franz Xaver Wortmann), MH 32, MH 45 and MH 60
(Martin Hepperle), RG-15 (Rolf Girsberger), S1223 and S5010 (Michael
Selig), SD7003, SD7037 and SD7062 (Michael Selig and John Donovan). The
files are unchanged apart from line endings; the airfoils remain the work
of their designers.

The reference results in tests/ were computed with XFOIL 6.996, built from
the official sources by the xfoil-reference workflow, and in tests/avl with
AVL 3.52, built by the avl-reference workflow; neither program is
distributed with this project.

The wind-tunnel measurements in tests/wind_tunnel come from NASA TM-4062
(McGhee, Walker and Millard, 1988), a work of the United States Government.

The application bundles three.js (https://threejs.org, MIT license) for its
3D view, along with its other npm dependencies listed in app/package.json.
